1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,007
Sweetheart, the winter after my husband had

2
00:00:03,007 --> 00:00:04,615
his first heart attack,

3
00:00:04,615 --> 00:00:07,762
a skinny eleven-year-old boy from the trailer

4
00:00:07,762 --> 00:00:11,049
court started shoveling our driveway every time

5
00:00:11,049 --> 00:00:11,749
it snowed.

6
00:00:11,929 --> 00:00:13,113
Before dawn.

7
00:00:13,293 --> 00:00:14,930
Without being asked.

8
00:00:15,110 --> 00:00:16,678
And he would never,

9
00:00:16,678 --> 00:00:18,163
ever take a penny.

10
00:00:18,343 --> 00:00:19,838
For seven winters,

11
00:00:19,838 --> 00:00:21,664
I never found out why.

12
00:00:21,844 --> 00:00:24,544
And then, twenty-five years later,

13
00:00:24,544 --> 00:00:27,404
on the coldest Christmas Eve in Ohio

14
00:00:27,404 --> 00:00:29,151
history, he came back,

15
00:00:29,151 --> 00:00:30,342
and he told me.

16
00:00:30,693 --> 00:00:33,049
Tonight I'm going to tell you about

17
00:00:33,049 --> 00:00:35,137
that boy, the one time I caught

18
00:00:35,137 --> 00:00:37,628
him, and the secret he'd been keeping

19
00:00:37,628 --> 00:00:39,446
since nineteen eighty-nine.

20
00:00:39,626 --> 00:00:40,657
Stay with me,

21
00:00:40,657 --> 00:00:43,827
honey, because the reason he shoveled my

22
00:00:43,827 --> 00:00:46,681
driveway is something I had done and

23
00:00:46,681 --> 00:00:48,346
completely forgotten.

24
00:00:48,526 --> 00:00:51,230
And it's the reason I believe nothing

25
00:00:51,230 --> 00:00:53,204
kind you ever do is wasted.

26
00:00:53,555 --> 00:00:55,927
In the fall of nineteen eighty-nine,

27
00:00:55,927 --> 00:00:58,035
Harold had a heart attack at the

28
00:00:58,035 --> 00:00:58,431
plant.

29
00:00:58,611 --> 00:01:00,268
He was only forty-seven,

30
00:01:00,268 --> 00:01:02,546
but he'd smoked for thirty years.

31
00:01:02,726 --> 00:01:05,098
He came home from the hospital with

32
00:01:05,098 --> 00:01:07,470
a bottle of pills and strict orders

33
00:01:07,470 --> 00:01:08,555
from the doctor.

34
00:01:08,735 --> 00:01:09,989
No smoking.

35
00:01:10,169 --> 00:01:11,492
No red meat.

36
00:01:11,672 --> 00:01:13,924
And no shoveling snow.

37
00:01:14,104 --> 00:01:15,573
Not one shovelful,

38
00:01:15,573 --> 00:01:16,879
the doctor said.

39
00:01:17,059 --> 00:01:19,592
Shoveling snow kills more men with bad

40
00:01:19,592 --> 00:01:22,191
hearts every winter than anything else.

41
00:01:22,540 --> 00:01:25,410
Now, Harold was a stubborn man,

42
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:25,965
honey.

43
00:01:26,145 --> 00:01:28,640
The first time it snowed that December,

44
00:01:28,640 --> 00:01:30,174
I woke up at five in the

45
00:01:30,174 --> 00:01:32,221
morning to the sound of a shovel

46
00:01:32,221 --> 00:01:33,820
scraping on the driveway.

47
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:35,775
And I flew out of bed,

48
00:01:35,775 --> 00:01:37,227
ready to kill him.

49
00:01:37,407 --> 00:01:40,205
But Harold was right there next to

50
00:01:40,205 --> 00:01:41,192
me, snoring.

51
00:01:41,372 --> 00:01:43,828
Somebody else was out there in the

52
00:01:43,828 --> 00:01:45,923
dark, shoveling our driveway.

53
00:01:46,103 --> 00:01:48,460
In a minute I'll tell you who.

54
00:01:48,809 --> 00:01:50,342
I ran to the window.

55
00:01:50,522 --> 00:01:52,837
And there, in the light from the

56
00:01:52,837 --> 00:01:55,441
streetlamp, was a little figure in a

57
00:01:55,441 --> 00:01:57,466
too-big coat and a knit cap,

58
00:01:57,466 --> 00:01:59,636
shoveling as fast as he could.

59
00:01:59,816 --> 00:02:01,456
By the time I got my robe

60
00:02:01,456 --> 00:02:03,359
on and opened the front door,

61
00:02:03,359 --> 00:02:04,146
he was gone.

62
00:02:04,326 --> 00:02:07,032
Running down the street toward the trailer

63
00:02:07,032 --> 00:02:08,320
court on Leppo Road.

64
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:10,793
Our driveway was clean,

65
00:02:10,793 --> 00:02:11,391
honey.

66
00:02:11,571 --> 00:02:12,697
Every inch.

67
00:02:12,877 --> 00:02:14,468
Even the front walk.

68
00:02:14,817 --> 00:02:17,756
It happened every time it snowed that

69
00:02:17,756 --> 00:02:18,312
winter.

70
00:02:18,492 --> 00:02:20,106
Every single time.

71
00:02:20,286 --> 00:02:21,671
Five in the morning,

72
00:02:21,671 --> 00:02:23,403
the scrape of the shovel,

73
00:02:23,403 --> 00:02:25,066
and by the time I got to

74
00:02:25,066 --> 00:02:26,590
the door, he was gone.

75
00:02:26,770 --> 00:02:28,662
Harold was mortified.

76
00:02:28,842 --> 00:02:30,333
He said, Ruthie,

77
00:02:30,333 --> 00:02:32,476
I'm not a charity case.

78
00:02:32,656 --> 00:02:34,880
Find out who it is and pay

79
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:35,222
him.

80
00:02:35,402 --> 00:02:37,539
So I left five dollars in an

81
00:02:37,539 --> 00:02:39,829
envelope taped to the mailbox,

82
00:02:39,829 --> 00:02:41,508
with a note that said,

83
00:02:41,508 --> 00:02:43,645
thank you, please take this.

84
00:02:43,995 --> 00:02:45,276
The next morning,

85
00:02:45,276 --> 00:02:47,235
the driveway was shoveled,

86
00:02:47,235 --> 00:02:49,720
and the envelope was still on the

87
00:02:49,720 --> 00:02:50,323
mailbox.

88
00:02:50,503 --> 00:02:52,778
With the five dollars inside.

89
00:02:52,958 --> 00:02:54,456
And under my note,

90
00:02:54,456 --> 00:02:57,119
in a child's pencil handwriting,

91
00:02:57,119 --> 00:02:58,949
it said, no thank you.

92
00:02:59,129 --> 00:03:00,674
And that was it,

93
00:03:00,674 --> 00:03:01,254
honey.

94
00:03:01,434 --> 00:03:03,587
Every winter after that,

95
00:03:03,587 --> 00:03:04,394
the same.

96
00:03:04,574 --> 00:03:06,711
I tried leaving cookies.

97
00:03:06,891 --> 00:03:07,819
Gone.

98
00:03:07,999 --> 00:03:10,170
Hot cocoa in a thermos.

99
00:03:10,350 --> 00:03:13,211
Gone, and the thermos washed and left

100
00:03:13,211 --> 00:03:14,217
on the porch.

101
00:03:14,397 --> 00:03:16,243
But money, never.

102
00:03:16,592 --> 00:03:19,387
If you're enjoying Grandma's story,

103
00:03:19,387 --> 00:03:21,542
tap the like button for me,

104
00:03:21,542 --> 00:03:22,421
sweetheart.

105
00:03:22,601 --> 00:03:25,782
It helps more people find these stories.

106
00:03:25,962 --> 00:03:28,295
One morning in the second winter,

107
00:03:28,295 --> 00:03:30,415
I hid behind the front door at

108
00:03:30,415 --> 00:03:32,395
four thirty with my coat on,

109
00:03:32,395 --> 00:03:34,374
and when I heard the shovel,

110
00:03:34,374 --> 00:03:35,505
I flung it open.

111
00:03:35,685 --> 00:03:37,564
And there he was,

112
00:03:37,564 --> 00:03:38,448
finally.

113
00:03:38,628 --> 00:03:41,195
A skinny boy with big brown eyes

114
00:03:41,195 --> 00:03:43,040
and a scar on his chin.

115
00:03:43,220 --> 00:03:44,962
He froze like a deer.

116
00:03:45,311 --> 00:03:46,624
I said, honey,

117
00:03:46,624 --> 00:03:47,750
who are you?

118
00:03:47,930 --> 00:03:49,694
Why are you doing this?

119
00:03:49,874 --> 00:03:50,993
And he said,

120
00:03:50,993 --> 00:03:52,392
my name's Tony,

121
00:03:52,392 --> 00:03:52,951
ma'am.

122
00:03:53,131 --> 00:03:54,604
And then he said,

123
00:03:54,604 --> 00:03:56,509
I just like shoveling.

124
00:03:56,689 --> 00:03:59,019
Which was the worst lie I've ever

125
00:03:59,019 --> 00:04:00,219
heard in my life.

126
00:04:00,399 --> 00:04:02,829
Nobody likes shoveling at five in the

127
00:04:02,829 --> 00:04:04,799
morning when it's ten degrees.

128
00:04:04,979 --> 00:04:06,164
I said, Tony,

129
00:04:06,164 --> 00:04:08,898
at least come in and have some

130
00:04:08,898 --> 00:04:09,809
breakfast.

131
00:04:09,989 --> 00:04:12,121
And he shook his head and ran

132
00:04:12,121 --> 00:04:12,415
off.

133
00:04:12,765 --> 00:04:14,414
So I asked around.

134
00:04:14,594 --> 00:04:15,848
Tony Russo.

135
00:04:16,028 --> 00:04:18,222
He lived in the trailer court on

136
00:04:18,222 --> 00:04:20,417
Leppo Road with his grandmother.

137
00:04:20,597 --> 00:04:22,234
His mother was gone,

138
00:04:22,234 --> 00:04:23,708
nobody knew where.

139
00:04:23,888 --> 00:04:26,877
His father was in prison in Mansfield,

140
00:04:26,877 --> 00:04:28,451
the big reformatory.

141
00:04:28,631 --> 00:04:30,251
He was a quiet kid,

142
00:04:30,251 --> 00:04:31,615
the school said.

143
00:04:31,795 --> 00:04:32,445
Smart.

144
00:04:32,625 --> 00:04:33,960
A little wild.

145
00:04:34,140 --> 00:04:36,706
Got in fights sometimes when the other

146
00:04:36,706 --> 00:04:38,935
boys teased him about his father.

147
00:04:39,115 --> 00:04:41,093
And he walked a mile each way

148
00:04:41,093 --> 00:04:43,480
in the snow to shovel our driveway.

149
00:04:43,830 --> 00:04:45,398
For seven winters,

150
00:04:45,398 --> 00:04:45,920
honey.

151
00:04:46,100 --> 00:04:47,958
From eleven to eighteen.

152
00:04:48,138 --> 00:04:50,567
Through Harold's second heart attack,

153
00:04:50,567 --> 00:04:52,143
and my mother's funeral,

154
00:04:52,143 --> 00:04:54,245
and the blizzard of ninety-four.

155
00:04:54,425 --> 00:04:56,922
Every snowfall, before dawn,

156
00:04:56,922 --> 00:04:58,082
without fail.

157
00:04:58,262 --> 00:05:01,456
Harold stopped being mortified after the first

158
00:05:01,456 --> 00:05:01,803
year.

159
00:05:01,983 --> 00:05:04,272
He'd lie in bed and listen to

160
00:05:04,272 --> 00:05:05,851
that shovel and say,

161
00:05:05,851 --> 00:05:07,115
there's our boy.

162
00:05:07,295 --> 00:05:10,383
And then, the winter Tony turned eighteen,

163
00:05:10,383 --> 00:05:11,927
the scraping stopped.

164
00:05:12,107 --> 00:05:13,697
He'd joined the Navy.

165
00:05:14,047 --> 00:05:16,572
Before he left for the Navy,

166
00:05:16,572 --> 00:05:18,645
honey, I did one thing.

167
00:05:18,825 --> 00:05:20,440
I knitted him a scarf,

168
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:22,348
navy blue, and I put it in

169
00:05:22,348 --> 00:05:24,036
a box with a note and a

170
00:05:24,036 --> 00:05:26,458
hundred dollars from our savings,

171
00:05:26,458 --> 00:05:28,954
and I took it to his grandmother's

172
00:05:28,954 --> 00:05:29,541
trailer.

173
00:05:29,721 --> 00:05:31,258
She came to the door,

174
00:05:31,258 --> 00:05:33,088
a tiny woman with a cane.

175
00:05:33,268 --> 00:05:35,439
She took the scarf and the note.

176
00:05:35,619 --> 00:05:37,941
But she handed me back the money.

177
00:05:38,121 --> 00:05:40,489
She said, he told me you'd try

178
00:05:40,489 --> 00:05:40,884
this.

179
00:05:41,064 --> 00:05:43,025
He said to tell you no thank

180
00:05:43,025 --> 00:05:43,305
you.

181
00:05:43,654 --> 00:05:45,754
So far, the heart attack,

182
00:05:45,754 --> 00:05:48,610
the envelope, and the boy with the

183
00:05:48,610 --> 00:05:49,030
scar.

184
00:05:49,210 --> 00:05:51,958
We never heard from Tony after that,

185
00:05:51,958 --> 00:05:52,797
sweetheart.

186
00:05:52,977 --> 00:05:55,532
Harold passed in two thousand eight.

187
00:05:55,712 --> 00:05:58,215
I wondered about Tony every winter,

188
00:05:58,215 --> 00:06:01,075
every time I heard a neighbor shoveling.

189
00:06:01,255 --> 00:06:02,428
Was he alive?

190
00:06:02,608 --> 00:06:04,071
Was he all right?

191
00:06:04,251 --> 00:06:05,775
Did he make it out of that

192
00:06:05,775 --> 00:06:06,596
trailer court?

193
00:06:06,776 --> 00:06:08,343
I prayed for him.

194
00:06:08,523 --> 00:06:09,377
And I never,

195
00:06:09,377 --> 00:06:11,797
ever figured out why he'd done it.

196
00:06:12,147 --> 00:06:14,992
Christmas Eve, two thousand fourteen.

197
00:06:15,172 --> 00:06:17,024
It was nine below zero,

198
00:06:17,024 --> 00:06:20,245
the coldest Christmas Eve anyone in Ohio

199
00:06:20,245 --> 00:06:21,453
could remember.

200
00:06:21,633 --> 00:06:24,652
And at five o'clock in the morning,

201
00:06:24,652 --> 00:06:26,377
I woke up in my bed,

202
00:06:26,377 --> 00:06:27,930
seventy years old,

203
00:06:27,930 --> 00:06:30,345
to a sound I hadn't heard in

204
00:06:30,345 --> 00:06:31,467
twenty years.

205
00:06:31,647 --> 00:06:33,790
The scrape of a shovel on my

206
00:06:33,790 --> 00:06:34,479
driveway.

207
00:06:34,659 --> 00:06:35,854
And I lay there,

208
00:06:35,854 --> 00:06:38,019
and I thought I was dreaming,

209
00:06:38,019 --> 00:06:38,467
honey.

210
00:06:38,647 --> 00:06:41,120
I thought it was Harold's ghost.

211
00:06:41,470 --> 00:06:43,360
I put on my robe and went

212
00:06:43,360 --> 00:06:44,267
to the door,

213
00:06:44,267 --> 00:06:45,325
and opened it.

214
00:06:45,505 --> 00:06:47,681
And there in the streetlight,

215
00:06:47,681 --> 00:06:49,332
shoveling my driveway,

216
00:06:49,332 --> 00:06:51,058
was a big man in a Navy

217
00:06:51,058 --> 00:06:53,609
peacoat, with gray at his temples.

218
00:06:53,789 --> 00:06:56,134
He stopped and turned around.

219
00:06:56,314 --> 00:06:58,299
He had a scar on his chin.

220
00:06:58,479 --> 00:06:59,806
And he said,

221
00:06:59,806 --> 00:07:01,242
morning, Mrs.

222
00:07:01,422 --> 00:07:02,247
Miller.

223
00:07:02,427 --> 00:07:04,955
I heard you've been doing your own

224
00:07:04,955 --> 00:07:06,293
driveway since Mr.

225
00:07:06,473 --> 00:07:07,738
Miller passed.

226
00:07:07,918 --> 00:07:09,300
That's not right.

227
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:10,862
And he smiled.

228
00:07:11,211 --> 00:07:13,291
I made him come in this time,

229
00:07:13,291 --> 00:07:14,079
sweetheart.

230
00:07:14,259 --> 00:07:16,511
I didn't give him a choice.

231
00:07:16,691 --> 00:07:19,215
I sat him down in Harold's chair

232
00:07:19,215 --> 00:07:21,345
in my kitchen with a cup of

233
00:07:21,345 --> 00:07:23,474
coffee and a plate of eggs,

234
00:07:23,474 --> 00:07:24,342
and I said,

235
00:07:24,342 --> 00:07:26,944
Tony Russo, you are going to tell

236
00:07:26,944 --> 00:07:28,364
me why, right now,

237
00:07:28,364 --> 00:07:30,414
or I'm calling the police.

238
00:07:30,594 --> 00:07:31,704
And he laughed,

239
00:07:31,704 --> 00:07:33,701
and then he got very quiet,

240
00:07:33,701 --> 00:07:35,698
and he took out his wallet,

241
00:07:35,698 --> 00:07:38,138
and he unfolded a little piece of

242
00:07:38,138 --> 00:07:38,582
paper.

243
00:07:38,932 --> 00:07:40,485
It was a note,

244
00:07:40,485 --> 00:07:41,150
honey.

245
00:07:41,330 --> 00:07:44,325
Old, soft, falling apart.

246
00:07:44,505 --> 00:07:46,700
And it was in my handwriting.

247
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:49,236
It said, To the little boy at

248
00:07:49,236 --> 00:07:50,293
the bus stop.

249
00:07:50,473 --> 00:07:52,470
Your daddy made a mistake.

250
00:07:52,650 --> 00:07:54,531
That doesn't make you one.

251
00:07:54,711 --> 00:07:56,278
You are a good boy.

252
00:07:56,458 --> 00:07:57,944
Merry Christmas.

253
00:07:58,124 --> 00:08:00,303
And taped to it was a two-dollar

254
00:08:00,303 --> 00:08:00,643
bill.

255
00:08:00,823 --> 00:08:02,982
I'd completely forgotten it,

256
00:08:02,982 --> 00:08:03,830
sweetheart.

257
00:08:04,010 --> 00:08:06,385
I stared at my own handwriting and

258
00:08:06,385 --> 00:08:08,411
couldn't remember writing it.

259
00:08:08,760 --> 00:08:10,200
Tony told me.

260
00:08:10,380 --> 00:08:12,949
In December of nineteen eighty-eight,

261
00:08:12,949 --> 00:08:14,060
when he was ten,

262
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:15,865
a group of boys at the bus

263
00:08:15,865 --> 00:08:18,782
stop by the five-and-dime were teasing him

264
00:08:18,782 --> 00:08:21,073
about his father being in prison.

265
00:08:21,253 --> 00:08:23,285
They pushed him down in the slush.

266
00:08:23,465 --> 00:08:25,375
And a lady in a red coat,

267
00:08:25,375 --> 00:08:26,904
waiting for the bus,

268
00:08:26,904 --> 00:08:29,502
chased them off with her umbrella.

269
00:08:29,682 --> 00:08:31,751
She gave him a tissue for his

270
00:08:31,751 --> 00:08:32,608
bloody chin.

271
00:08:32,788 --> 00:08:35,078
And before she got on her bus,

272
00:08:35,078 --> 00:08:37,826
she pressed that note into his hand.

273
00:08:38,176 --> 00:08:39,982
I remembered it then,

274
00:08:39,982 --> 00:08:40,498
honey.

275
00:08:40,678 --> 00:08:41,491
Barely.

276
00:08:41,671 --> 00:08:43,835
I'd written it on the back of

277
00:08:43,835 --> 00:08:44,954
a grocery list,

278
00:08:44,954 --> 00:08:45,775
on the bus,

279
00:08:45,775 --> 00:08:48,536
with a little two-dollar bill from my

280
00:08:48,536 --> 00:08:49,357
coin purse.

281
00:08:49,537 --> 00:08:51,778
I'd never seen the boy again.

282
00:08:51,958 --> 00:08:53,873
Tony said, Mrs.

283
00:08:54,053 --> 00:08:57,015
Miller, I read that note every night

284
00:08:57,015 --> 00:08:57,919
for a year.

285
00:08:58,099 --> 00:09:00,944
Everybody else said I'd end up like

286
00:09:00,944 --> 00:09:01,513
my dad.

287
00:09:01,693 --> 00:09:03,812
You were the only person who ever

288
00:09:03,812 --> 00:09:04,839
said I was good.

289
00:09:05,019 --> 00:09:06,988
And then I saw your name on

290
00:09:06,988 --> 00:09:07,864
the mailbox.

291
00:09:08,044 --> 00:09:09,971
And your husband was sick.

292
00:09:10,320 --> 00:09:12,596
Harold knew, sweetheart.

293
00:09:12,776 --> 00:09:15,423
That's the other thing Tony told me.

294
00:09:15,603 --> 00:09:18,058
One morning in the third winter,

295
00:09:18,058 --> 00:09:20,283
Harold had gotten up at five,

296
00:09:20,283 --> 00:09:21,741
without telling me,

297
00:09:21,741 --> 00:09:23,890
and gone out on the porch in

298
00:09:23,890 --> 00:09:26,191
his bathrobe, and caught Tony.

299
00:09:26,371 --> 00:09:28,949
And Tony told him the whole story.

300
00:09:29,129 --> 00:09:31,688
And Harold made him promise to keep

301
00:09:31,688 --> 00:09:34,028
shoveling, and never to tell me.

302
00:09:34,208 --> 00:09:36,472
He said, let her wonder.

303
00:09:36,652 --> 00:09:38,812
It'll do her good to get something

304
00:09:38,812 --> 00:09:40,019
she can't pay back.

305
00:09:40,369 --> 00:09:43,691
Harold kept that secret for eighteen years,

306
00:09:43,691 --> 00:09:45,931
honey, until the day he died.

307
00:09:46,111 --> 00:09:49,266
My Harold, who couldn't keep a surprise

308
00:09:49,266 --> 00:09:51,370
party secret for two days.

309
00:09:51,550 --> 00:09:53,813
I sat at that kitchen table and

310
00:09:53,813 --> 00:09:56,368
laughed and cried at the same time.

311
00:09:56,548 --> 00:09:58,475
Tony said, Mr.

312
00:09:58,655 --> 00:10:00,724
Miller used to bring me a cup

313
00:10:00,724 --> 00:10:03,362
of coffee on the porch every morning,

314
00:10:03,362 --> 00:10:04,646
before your cocoa.

315
00:10:04,826 --> 00:10:07,160
Black, with one sugar.

316
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:09,418
He got up at four thirty to

317
00:10:09,418 --> 00:10:10,033
make it.

318
00:10:10,383 --> 00:10:13,033
Tony spent twenty years in the Navy,

319
00:10:13,033 --> 00:10:13,843
sweetheart.

320
00:10:14,023 --> 00:10:16,519
He became a chief petty officer.

321
00:10:16,699 --> 00:10:17,900
When he got out,

322
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:20,152
he went to school and became a

323
00:10:20,152 --> 00:10:23,454
counselor, at a juvenile detention center in

324
00:10:23,454 --> 00:10:24,130
Columbus.

325
00:10:24,310 --> 00:10:26,783
For boys whose fathers are in prison.

326
00:10:26,963 --> 00:10:29,424
Boys exactly like he was.

327
00:10:29,604 --> 00:10:31,473
He said, I tell every one of

328
00:10:31,473 --> 00:10:33,609
them the same thing you told me.

329
00:10:33,789 --> 00:10:35,798
Your daddy made a mistake.

330
00:10:35,978 --> 00:10:37,766
That doesn't make you one.

331
00:10:38,115 --> 00:10:40,183
And then he told me why he'd

332
00:10:40,183 --> 00:10:42,841
come back that particular Christmas.

333
00:10:43,021 --> 00:10:45,277
His own father had just died,

334
00:10:45,277 --> 00:10:47,456
in prison, the month before.

335
00:10:47,636 --> 00:10:49,652
Tony had gone to see him at

336
00:10:49,652 --> 00:10:51,892
the end, for the first time in

337
00:10:51,892 --> 00:10:54,207
thirty years, and forgiven him.

338
00:10:54,387 --> 00:10:56,027
And on the drive home,

339
00:10:56,027 --> 00:10:57,964
he said, he thought of me,

340
00:10:57,964 --> 00:10:59,082
and the shovel,

341
00:10:59,082 --> 00:10:59,902
and Harold.

342
00:11:00,082 --> 00:11:02,452
And he couldn't stand the thought of

343
00:11:02,452 --> 00:11:05,086
me shoveling my own driveway at seventy.

344
00:11:05,435 --> 00:11:08,217
Tony lives in Mansfield now,

345
00:11:08,217 --> 00:11:10,602
honey, ten minutes away.

346
00:11:10,782 --> 00:11:13,348
He's married, with two boys.

347
00:11:13,528 --> 00:11:15,333
And every time it snows,

348
00:11:15,333 --> 00:11:17,064
at five in the morning,

349
00:11:17,064 --> 00:11:19,321
I hear the scrape of a shovel.

350
00:11:19,501 --> 00:11:21,611
Now it's his boys doing it,

351
00:11:21,611 --> 00:11:24,269
and Tony supervising with a cup of

352
00:11:24,269 --> 00:11:25,051
my coffee.

353
00:11:25,231 --> 00:11:27,460
They never take a penny either.

354
00:11:27,640 --> 00:11:29,300
I've stopped trying.

355
00:11:29,480 --> 00:11:31,778
Some debts, you just have to let

356
00:11:31,778 --> 00:11:32,568
people pay.

357
00:11:32,918 --> 00:11:35,701
Here's what I want you to remember,

358
00:11:35,701 --> 00:11:36,576
sweetheart.

359
00:11:36,756 --> 00:11:39,434
I spent two dollars and thirty seconds

360
00:11:39,434 --> 00:11:41,126
on a crying boy at a bus

361
00:11:41,126 --> 00:11:43,241
stop, and I forgot about it by

362
00:11:43,241 --> 00:11:44,650
the time I got home.

363
00:11:44,830 --> 00:11:47,489
He carried it for twenty-six years.

364
00:11:47,669 --> 00:11:50,342
You never know which kindness will be

365
00:11:50,342 --> 00:11:50,920
the one.

366
00:11:51,100 --> 00:11:52,937
Tell me in the comments,

367
00:11:52,937 --> 00:11:56,151
honey, has a stranger's words ever changed

368
00:11:56,151 --> 00:11:56,916
your life?

369
00:11:57,096 --> 00:11:59,233
There are more of my family's stories

370
00:11:59,233 --> 00:12:01,255
in the playlist right there on your

371
00:12:01,255 --> 00:12:01,659
screen.

372
00:12:01,839 --> 00:12:02,861
Good night.
